[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. It was not one issue.
Its reports hold more than one problem, and each now has its own ticket.

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed (`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu
locales`), and the booking-v3 form is retired, so its hint won't be fixed.

<Callout kind="warn">
Closing #4471 did not fix the one-day moves. Neither change touches them, so their ticket stays
open until something does.
</Callout>

## The problems

<CauseTickets ticket={4471} />

Counted once per report id: the export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.

## What each one is

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants write the day
  first, so day and month swap: 7 April becomes 4 July. Every tenant, through the app, email
  and the booking-v2 form.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the date
  day first, the tenant's way. People type what the hint says, and day and month swap. Same
  symptom as `parser`, different code, different fix: only the form of booking-v3 tenants.
- **one-day move**: the date moves by one day, or one meeting shows on different days for people
  in different places. No swap explains it. It points at a time-zone conversion, not at the
  parser. The desk read these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) and still files them under
  their tenant's cause, so they count there too; their own ticket tracks the move.

## How the split was made

By the rules in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts`: the tenant's locale and form decide the cause, not
the intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and on #4471 it was wrong both
ways. Merging by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) put
every problem into one ticket, because the reports all say the same thing: wrong day.

## Lessons

- The same complaint is not the same bug. Before a big ticket is marked known, sort it by
  cause, not by subject.
- A report the cause rules cannot place needs a person to read it, and its own count.

